\section{What this does to your results}{

Neighbourhoods overlap, so smoothing over them makes nearby cells resemble
each other in every gene at once. Gene-gene correlation after imputation is
inflated, badly, and the inflation is a property of the graph rather than of
the biology. Do not feed imputed values into Hotspot, SCENIC, differential
correlation or CoReMo: those methods measure exactly the quantity MAGIC
manufactures. Visualisation and trend fitting are what it is for.

The operator preserves per-cell mass, so imputed values sit on the scale of
whatever went in. Imputing raw counts and imputing log-normalised counts are
different operations rather than the same one rescaled, see the \code{layer}
element of \code{\link[=params_sc_magic]{params_sc_magic()}}.
